@page "/chat"
@attribute [Authorize]
@inject ICurrentUser Caller
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@* AD-13's clock rather than the ambient one, for the day separators: "today" is a claim about the
   present, and a screen that read DateTimeOffset.Now would be the one place in the product that
   answered it from a clock no test can set. The zone it is reckoned in is the host's - this is a
   Blazor Server circuit, so nothing here can see the browser's offset without asking for it. *@
@inject TimeProvider Clock
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@implements IAsyncDisposable

@* FR-68 to FR-76: the one chat screen, for both of its participants.

   A driver sees their own conversation and no picker - they have exactly one thread and no reason
   to choose between them. A dispatcher sees the roster of drivers beside the conversation they
   picked. A client or an administrator sees neither: chat has two participants by product decision,
   and an admin is deliberately not one of them.

   FR-12, and it is the part worth repeating: what is rendered here is a convenience. IAccessGuard
   inside IChatService is what refuses a caller, and it runs whether or not this screen offered them
   anything - which is why [Authorize] carries no role and there is no <AuthorizeView> below.

   Every branch below is a call into ChatViews. The decisions are there rather than here because a
   statically rendered component dispatches no events: a rule written inside an @onclick handler or
   behind a live socket is a rule no test in this solution can reach (DW-17, DW-18).

   The connection lives in the browser, not in the circuit. A server-side HubConnection would carry
   no session cookie and would need a token the application minted for itself; the browser already
   holds drivetrack.session, and the hub sits outside /api, so it connects with nothing added.

   Independent @if blocks and one literal localizer key per string, for the reasons the other
   screens state: an `else` at markup level reads to NFR-14's scan as a rendered English word, and a
   key composed at run time is a key the closed-catalogue test cannot see. *@

<PageTitle>@Localizer["ChatTitle"]</PageTitle>

@* One reading of the clock for the whole render, handed to every day separator below. Read per
   branch instead, a midnight ticking between two of the three questions a separator asks renders
   that separator carrying two labels or none at all.

   At the top of the markup rather than beside the list it serves, and that is forced: a bare
   statement inside a `@@if` block is markup as far as NFR-14's scan is concerned, and it reads
   `var now = Now;` as three rendered English words. A `@@{ }` block is code the scan removes, and
   Razor only accepts one where it is not already inside a code block. *@
@{ var now = Now; }

@* The screen is a column that fills what the shell gives it, and the card takes whatever is left
   after the heading and the banner. That is what the column is for: the card used to be a fixed
   `100dvh` less a figure tuned to the heading alone, so the first refusal - which this screen
   deliberately renders above the card - pushed the composer off the bottom of the viewport, which
   is the exact failure a bounded card exists to prevent. *@
<div class="dt-chat-screen">
    <div class="dt-page-head mb-4">
        <h1 class="dt-type-h1">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Chat" />@Localizer["ChatTitle"]
        </h1>
    </div>

    @* Above the card rather than inside it. Every refusal the hub can answer with arrives here -
       including CHAT_MESSAGE_TOO_LONG, which is about the line still sitting in the composer - and a
       banner inside a panel that scrolls is a banner that can be scrolled out of sight. *@
    <DtFailureBanner Failures="_failures" />

    @if (!_isParticipant)
    {
        <p class="dt-chat-empty" role="status">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Forbidden" />@Localizer["ChatNotParticipant"]
        </p>
    }

    @if (_isParticipant)
    {
        @* The hub is down, and this is the only thing that can say so. The module reconnects in the
           background and says nothing while it does, so a driver at a doorstep goes on typing into a
           connection that is gone and finds out when Send fails.

           Outside the conversation rather than inside it, and that is the whole point of where it
           sits: a dispatcher who has not picked anyone yet has no conversation on screen, and a dock
           nested in one would leave the roster looking perfectly live while nothing it opened could
           ever load.

           A different failure from the circuit's, which MainLayout's own dock speaks for: the page is
           alive, the conversation is not. Nothing here is a copy of that dock - they are two
           failures, and a caller shown one of them learns nothing about the other. *@
        @if (_hubDown)
        {
            <p class="dt-chat-dock" role="alert">
                <span class="dt-chat-dock__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                @* Still retrying, and saying so. *@
                @if (!_hubGaveUp)
                {
                    <span class="dt-chat-dock__text">@Localizer["ChatDisconnected"]</span>
                }
                @* The retries have stopped, so the sentence stops promising a reconnection that is
                   not coming and offers the one way out there is. Without this the screen sits under
                   "reconnecting…" for the rest of the session. *@
                @if (_hubGaveUp)
                {
                    <span class="dt-chat-dock__text">@Localizer["ChatConnectionLost"]</span>
                    <a href="." class="dt-chat-dock__action">@Localizer["Reload"]</a>
                }
            </p>
        }

        <div class="dt-chat">
            @if (_showsRoster)
            {
                <nav class="dt-chat-roster" aria-label="@Localizer["ChatThreads"]">
                    @* The search stays put while the roster scrolls under it. A fleet is longer than the
                       panel, and a box that scrolled away with the rows would be a box a dispatcher has
                       to scroll back up to reach every time they narrow the list. No visible label: the
                       panel is already named "Водії" by the nav around it, and a second heading directly
                       under the first names the same list twice. *@
                    <div class="dt-chat-search">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.Search" />
                        <input id="chat-roster-search"
                               type="search"
                               class="form-control dt-chat-search__box"
                               @bind="_search"
                               @bind:event="oninput"
                               placeholder="@Localizer["ChatFindDriver"]"
                               aria-label="@Localizer["ChatFindDriver"]" />
                    </div>

                    @* A list, because it is one: a screen reader is told how many conversations there
                       are before it starts reading names, which a run of loose buttons cannot say.

                       Keyed on the driver, because the membership of this list changes on every
                       keystroke in the box above: diffed by position, Blazor would hand row three's
                       component the state of whatever used to be third and a dispatcher would watch
                       names swap under the cursor as they typed. *@
                    <ul class="dt-chat-threads">
                        @foreach (var summary in VisibleThreads)
                        {
                            <ChatRosterRow @key="summary.DriverId"
                                           Summary="summary"
                                           OpenDriverId="_thread?.DriverId"
                                           OnOpen="Open(summary)" />
                        }
                    </ul>

                    @* A filter that matched nobody says so. A blank column under a box somebody has
                       just typed into reads as a roster that failed to load, and the only way to tell
                       the two apart is to clear the box and see whether anything comes back. *@
                    @if (FilterFoundNothing)
                    {
                        <p class="dt-chat-empty" role="status">@Localizer["ChatNoDriverFound"]</p>
                    }
                </nav>
            }

            <section class="dt-chat-thread-panel">
                @* The header names who the caller is talking to, which is a different person on each
                   side of the same conversation. *@
                <h2 class="dt-chat-header dt-type-h2">
                    @if (ChatViews.NamesTheDispatchDesk(_role))
                    {
                        <span>@Localizer["ChatDispatchDesk"]</span>
                    }
                    @if (ChatViews.NamesTheSelectedDriver(_role, _thread is not null))
                    {
                        <span>@SelectedDriverName</span>
                    }
                    @if (ChatViews.AsksForAThread(_role, _thread is not null))
                    {
                        <span>@Localizer["ChatThreads"]</span>
                    }
                </h2>

                @if (ChatViews.AsksForAThread(_role, _thread is not null))
                {
                    <p class="dt-chat-empty" role="status">@Localizer["ChatSelectThread"]</p>
                }

                @if (_thread is not null)
                {
                    @* aria-live, because a message that arrives while the caller is reading something
                       else is announced rather than silently appended. *@
                    <ol id="@_listId"
                        class="dt-chat-messages"
                        aria-live="polite"
                        aria-label="@Localizer["ChatConversation"]">
                        @* Over positions rather than over the lines themselves, because the grouping
                           decision needs the line before this one and an element alone cannot name it.
                           The three helpers below are one line each: what the markup can name here is a
                           position, and turning that position into the instants the decision takes is
                           the whole of their job.

                           A range rather than a `for` header, and the reason is NFR-14's scan: a `<` in
                           markup-level C# opens a tag as far as that scan is concerned, and everything
                           in front of it is then read as rendered English.

                           Both children are keyed, because this list interleaves two component types
                           and grows from the top as history is rejoined: diffed by position, Blazor
                           would hand a bubble the state of the separator that used to sit at its index.
                           The keys cannot collide - a message id is an int and a separator's is the
                           day it opens. *@
                        @foreach (var index in Enumerable.Range(0, _thread.Messages.Count))
                        {
                            @if (StartsADay(index))
                            {
                                <ChatDaySeparator @key="DayOf(index)" SentAt="SentAt(index)" Now="now" />
                            }

                            <ChatLine @key="Line(index).Id" Message="Line(index)" ViewerUserId="_callerUserId" />
                        }
                    </ol>

                    @if (_thread.Messages.Count == 0)
                    {
                        <p class="dt-chat-empty" role="status">@Localizer["ChatNoMessages"]</p>
                    }

                    @* A form rather than a div, so Enter sends. The default submission is prevented
                       because nothing is posted: the message goes over the hub, and a GET to this same
                       address would take the conversation off the screen. *@
                    <form class="dt-chat-composer" @onsubmit="SendAsync" @onsubmit:preventDefault>
                        <input class="form-control dt-chat-input"
                               @bind="_draft"
                               @bind:event="oninput"
                               placeholder="@Localizer["ChatMessagePlaceholder"]"
                               aria-label="@Localizer["ChatMessagePlaceholder"]" />
                        @* The hub-down hold is a fieldset around the control rather than a second flag
                           in its own `disabled` binding, and the two are deliberately separate: one is
                           "this send is already running", the other is "there is nothing to send into".
                           A composite binding would have merged two failures into one word, and the box
                           above stays live either way - what was typed while the hub was down is still
                           there to send when it comes back. *@
                        <fieldset class="dt-chat-hold" disabled="@_hubDown">
                            @* Disabled while a send is in flight: a double-click must not persist two
                               rows, and there is no dedupe anywhere downstream to undo one that did. *@
                            <button type="submit"
                                    class="btn btn-primary dt-chat-send"
                                    disabled="@_sending">
                                <Icon Name="IconName.Send" />@Localizer["ChatSend"]
                            </button>
                        </fieldset>
                    </form>
                }
            </section>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The path the collocated module is served from — the plain path rather than a fingerprinted
    /// one, for the reason DtDialog gives: `Assets[...]` resolves a hashed href for a declarative
    /// script tag, and `import()` from C# can only name this.
    /// </summary>
    private const string ModulePath = "./Components/Pages/Chat.razor.js";

    /// <summary>
    /// Generated, because the module scrolls the list by element id and two screens on one circuit
    /// must not answer to the same one.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly string _listId = $"dt-chat-messages-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    private IJSObjectReference? _module;
    private IJSObjectReference? _handle;
    private DotNetObjectReference<Chat>? _self;

    private IReadOnlyList<ChatThreadSummaryView> _threads = [];
    private ChatThreadView? _thread;
    private IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> _failures = [];
    private string? _draft;
    private string _search = string.Empty;
    private bool _sending;

    /// <summary>
    /// True while the hub is unreachable — between a drop and the reconnect that answers it, or for
    /// good once the retries have given up.
    /// <para>
    /// Its own flag rather than a reading of the connection, because there is nothing on this side
    /// to read: the module owns the socket and the screen learns what happened to it only when it
    /// is told (<see cref="ConnectionChangedAsync"/>). Nothing clears it but that callback, so a
    /// dock that is showing is a hub that has not come back.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool _hubDown;

    /// <summary>
    /// True once the connection has stopped trying — the retries exhausted, or a first connect that
    /// never opened at all.
    /// <para>
    /// Separate from <see cref="_hubDown"/> because the two states need two different sentences. A
    /// drop the client is still working on is a wait; a drop it has given up on is not, and a screen
    /// that goes on saying "reconnecting…" for the rest of the session is a screen lying to someone
    /// who has no way out of it. This is what puts a Reload in the dock, exactly as
    /// <c>DtConnectionDock</c> carries one for the circuit's own version of this.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool _hubGaveUp;

    private UserRole _role;
    private bool _isParticipant;
    private bool _showsRoster;
    private int _callerUserId;

    /// <summary>
    /// The name the header shows a dispatcher. A property rather than <c>@_thread!.DriverName</c> in
    /// the markup: the null-forgiving operator is not part of the Razor expression grammar NFR-14's
    /// scan parses, so everything after it reads to that scan as rendered English.
    /// </summary>
    private string SelectedDriverName => _thread?.DriverName ?? string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// The present the day separators are named against, read fresh on every render rather than once
    /// at initialization. A circuit outlives a day: a screen left open overnight would otherwise go
    /// on labelling yesterday's separator "today" until it was reloaded.
    /// <para>
    /// Read once per render and handed down to every separator, because reading it per branch lets a
    /// midnight between two of the three questions render a separator with two labels or with none.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private DateTimeOffset Now => Clock.GetUtcNow();

    /// <summary>
    /// The roster rows the search leaves standing. Over the list the hub already answered with —
    /// typing here is never a query, exactly as the fleet screen's own search is not.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyCollection<ChatThreadSummaryView> VisibleThreads =>
        [.. _threads.Where(IsVisible)];

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a row is on the roster: the open conversation always, and anything the search matches.
    /// <para>
    /// The open one is exempt on purpose. Filtered out, the panel on the right would be showing a
    /// conversation with no row anywhere to the left of it and no <c>aria-current</c> on the page at
    /// all — a reader moving by list would be told nothing is open while they are reading it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsVisible(ChatThreadSummaryView summary) =>
        summary.DriverId == _thread?.DriverId || Matches(summary, _search);

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the search excluded every row there was. Distinguished from a roster that is empty
    /// because the fleet is: only the first is something the reader did, and only the first has an
    /// answer they can act on.
    /// </summary>
    private bool FilterFoundNothing => _threads.Count > 0 && VisibleThreads.Count == 0;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a roster row survives what a dispatcher typed: the driver's name, the vehicle they
    /// drive, or its plate.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="summary">The row being judged.</param>
    /// <param name="search">What was typed, or null and empty for "everything".</param>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Internal and static so a test can reach it. The screen's own roster arrives over a live hub
    /// connection, so a static render never holds a row to filter and the rule would otherwise be
    /// unreachable — the same reason every decision in <c>ChatViews</c> was lifted out.
    /// </remarks>
    internal static bool Matches(ChatThreadSummaryView summary, string? search)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(summary);

        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(search))
        {
            return true;
        }

        var needle = search.Trim();

        return Contains(summary.DriverName, needle)
            || Contains(summary.VehicleModel, needle)
            || Contains(summary.VehicleLicensePlate, needle);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a roster field holds what was typed, with the two scripts' lookalike letters read as
    /// the same letter.
    /// <para>
    /// A Ukrainian licence plate is written in the twelve Cyrillic letters that have a Latin twin —
    /// АА1234ВС is Cyrillic А, А, В, С — and a dispatcher at a Latin keyboard types AA1234BC. The
    /// two strings are different code points, so no culture comparison equates them and the
    /// commonest search on this screen would match nothing at all.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static bool Contains(string? haystack, string needle) =>
        haystack is not null
        && Latinized(haystack).Contains(Latinized(needle), StringComparison.CurrentCultureIgnoreCase);

    /// <summary>
    /// The Cyrillic letters that are drawn the same as a Latin one, and the Latin letters they are
    /// read as. Upper case only: <see cref="Latinized"/> folds the case first, so one row covers
    /// both.
    /// </summary>
    private const string Lookalikes = "АВЕІКМНОРСТХ";

    /// <inheritdoc cref="Lookalikes" />
    private const string LatinTwins = "ABEIKMHOPCTX";

    /// <summary>
    /// A string with its lookalike Cyrillic letters rewritten as their Latin twins, upper-cased.
    /// Applied to both sides of every comparison, so the fold is a way of reading rather than a
    /// change to what is stored or shown.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Latinized(string value)
    {
        var folded = value.ToUpperInvariant();
        var builder = new System.Text.StringBuilder(folded.Length);

        foreach (var character in folded)
        {
            var twin = Lookalikes.IndexOf(character, StringComparison.Ordinal);

            builder.Append(twin < 0 ? character : LatinTwins[twin]);
        }

        return builder.ToString();
    }

    /// <summary>The line at a position in the open conversation.</summary>
    private ChatMessageView Line(int index) => _thread!.Messages[index];

    /// <summary>
    /// When the line before this one was sent, or null when this is the first. The null is what
    /// makes the oldest day of a conversation a named day like every other.
    /// </summary>
    private DateTimeOffset? SentBefore(int index) =>
        index > 0 ? _thread!.Messages[index - 1].SentAt : null;

    /// <summary>When the line at a position was sent.</summary>
    private DateTimeOffset SentAt(int index) => Line(index).SentAt;

    /// <summary>Whether a separator is drawn above this line, through the decision that decides it.</summary>
    private bool StartsADay(int index) =>
        ChatViews.StartsADay(SentBefore(index), SentAt(index));

    /// <summary>
    /// The day a separator opens, which is also the key it is diffed by: unique among the separators
    /// in one conversation by construction, and of a type a message id can never equal.
    /// </summary>
    private DateOnly DayOf(int index) =>
        DateOnly.FromDateTime(SentAt(index).ToLocalTime().Date);

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // The circuit outlives the request that opened it, so the cookie can be gone by the time a
        // screen reads the caller. Raising the contract's own code here is what puts that case
        // through FR-13's single boundary instead of a per-screen redirect.
        if (!Caller.IsAuthenticated)
        {
            throw new ForbiddenException(
                ErrorCode.AUTH_UNAUTHENTICATED,
                "The circuit has no authenticated caller: the session cookie is gone.");
        }

        // Read once, after the authenticated check: ICurrentUser.Role throws for an anonymous
        // caller, so asking outside that branch would take the screen down instead of refusing it.
        _role = Caller.Role;
        _isParticipant = ChatViews.IsParticipant(_role);
        _showsRoster = ChatViews.ShowsRoster(_role);
        _callerUserId = Caller.UserId.Value;
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        // Not on every render, and not for a caller with no conversation to open: a client who
        // lands here is shown the refusal and never opens a socket.
        if (!firstRender || !_isParticipant)
        {
            return;
        }

        _self = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);

        try
        {
            // The import is inside the guard, not before it. A module that fails to load throws the
            // same JSException the calls below do, and an unhandled one out of OnAfterRenderAsync
            // tears down the circuit - which is the failure this catch exists to prevent.
            _module = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", ModulePath);

            _handle = await _module.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("connect", _self);

            if (_showsRoster)
            {
                _threads = await _module.InvokeAsync<ChatThreadSummaryView[]>("listThreads", _handle);
            }

            // FR-69: a driver's own conversation is the screen. Nothing to pick, so nothing to pick
            // from.
            if (!_showsRoster && Caller.DriverId is { } own)
            {
                await OpenAsync(own.Value);
            }
        }
        catch (JSException failure)
        {
            // A connect that never answered is a hub that is down, and down for good: there is no
            // connection to retry, so nothing will ever call back to clear this. Only when the
            // handle is still null, because the same catch covers a roster or a join that the
            // service refused over a connection that opened perfectly well - a refusal is not an
            // outage, and holding the composer shut for one would be the wrong answer to it.
            _hubDown = _handle is null;
            _hubGaveUp = _hubDown;

            _failures = [new ScreenFailure(null, KeyOf(failure))];
        }

        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>Called by the module when the hub broadcasts a message into a joined thread.</summary>
    /// <param name="driverId">The conversation the message belongs to.</param>
    /// <param name="message">The row the server stored.</param>
    [JSInvokable]
    public async Task ReceiveMessageAsync(int driverId, ChatMessageView message)
    {
        // A dispatcher who has switched conversations may still be in the old group for an instant,
        // so the conversation on screen is what decides whether this line belongs to it.
        if (_thread is null || _thread.DriverId != driverId)
        {
            return;
        }

        // By id, because a reconnect replays the whole rejoined conversation through here to
        // recover the lines that were broadcast while the connection was down. Every line already
        // on screen arrives a second time, and appending it would show the conversation twice.
        if (_thread.Messages.Any(line => line.Id == message.Id))
        {
            return;
        }

        // Asked before the line is appended, because the question is where the reader already was.
        // A dispatcher scrolled up into last week is reading; pulling them to the newest line
        // because somebody typed takes the page away mid-sentence. A reader already at the bottom is
        // following the conversation, and for them the newest line is the whole point (FR-74).
        var following = await IsAtBottomAsync();

        // Read again, because the await above yielded the circuit: a dispatcher who picked another
        // driver or left the screen while the browser was answering leaves `_thread` null or holding
        // a different conversation, and appending here would either take the circuit down inside a
        // [JSInvokable] or file the line under somebody else's name.
        if (_thread is null || _thread.DriverId != driverId)
        {
            return;
        }

        _thread = _thread with { Messages = [.. _thread.Messages, message] };

        StateHasChanged();

        if (!following)
        {
            return;
        }

        await ScrollAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Called by the module when the hub connection drops, gives up, or comes back (FR-75).
    /// <para>
    /// The one thing that can say the conversation is dead. <c>withAutomaticReconnect()</c> retries
    /// in the background and announces nothing, so without this a driver at a doorstep goes on
    /// typing into a socket that is gone and finds out when Send fails — by which time the message
    /// they meant to send is a refusal banner instead of a line in the conversation.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The draft is deliberately untouched in both directions. What was typed while the hub was down
    /// is what the sender still means to say when it comes back.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="connected">True when the hub is reachable again, false when it is not.</param>
    /// <param name="recoverable">
    /// Whether the client is still working on it. False is the retries having stopped, which is a
    /// different sentence and the one that has to offer a way out.
    /// </param>
    [JSInvokable]
    public Task ConnectionChangedAsync(bool connected, bool recoverable)
    {
        _hubDown = !connected;
        _hubGaveUp = !connected && !recoverable;

        // Invoked from the module rather than from a UI event, so nothing re-renders on its own.
        StateHasChanged();

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            if (_handle is not null)
            {
                if (_module is not null)
                {
                    // Stopped explicitly: a circuit that navigated away would otherwise leave a
                    // socket listening to a conversation nobody is reading.
                    await _module.InvokeVoidAsync("disconnect", _handle);
                }

                await _handle.DisposeAsync();
            }

            if (_module is not null)
            {
                await _module.DisposeAsync();
            }
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // The circuit went away before the component did; there is no browser left to tidy.
        }

        _self?.Dispose();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The handler for a roster entry, built in C# rather than written as a lambda in the attribute:
    /// a `=&gt;` there closes the tag as far as NFR-14's markup scan is concerned, and everything
    /// after it is read as rendered English.
    /// </summary>
    private Func<Task> Open(ChatThreadSummaryView summary) => () => OpenAsync(summary.DriverId);

    private async Task OpenAsync(int driverId)
    {
        if (_module is null || _handle is null)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            // Left before the new one is joined, so a dispatcher reading one driver's conversation
            // does not keep receiving another's in the background. Inside the guard, because a
            // JSException escaping here would tear down the circuit.
            if (_thread is not null && _thread.DriverId != driverId)
            {
                await _module.InvokeVoidAsync("leaveThread", _handle, _thread.DriverId);
            }

            _thread = await _module.InvokeAsync<ChatThreadView>("joinThread", _handle, driverId);
        }
        catch (JSException failure)
        {
            // Cleared, not left as it was. The old conversation has already been left, so keeping
            // it on screen would render a conversation this connection no longer receives - and
            // SendAsync would go on posting into it.
            _thread = null;
            _failures = [new ScreenFailure(null, KeyOf(failure))];

            StateHasChanged();

            return;
        }

        _failures = [];

        StateHasChanged();

        await ScrollAsync();
    }

    private async Task SendAsync()
    {
        // Enter in an empty box is not a send. Without this the hub refuses it, and the screen turns
        // a keystroke that posted nothing into a red banner about a required field.
        //
        // Its own statement rather than a clause in the guard below, because that guard is read by a
        // source scan that stops at the first closing parenthesis - a call with arguments inside it
        // would hide the re-entry check from the rule that exists to prove it is there.
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_draft))
        {
            return;
        }

        // `_hubDown` beside the in-flight flag rather than trusting the disabled control: the
        // attribute is a hint to the browser, and a keyboard activation or an Enter in the box
        // already dispatched reaches this handler whatever the button looked like.
        if (_module is null || _handle is null || _thread is null || _sending || _hubDown)
        {
            return;
        }

        // Set before the await and cleared in the finally: the button is disabled from the moment
        // the first click is handled until the server has answered, so a second click has nothing
        // to act on. There is no dedupe downstream, so two rows would simply be two messages.
        _sending = true;

        try
        {
            await _module.InvokeVoidAsync("send", _handle, _thread.DriverId, _draft);
        }
        catch (JSException failure)
        {
            // The draft stays in the box: a message the server refused for being too long is a
            // message the sender is being asked to shorten, not one to make them retype.
            _failures = [new ScreenFailure(null, KeyOf(failure))];

            return;
        }
        finally
        {
            _sending = false;
        }

        _failures = [];

        // FR-73: no optimistic echo. The box is cleared because the server accepted the message;
        // the line itself appears when the broadcast arrives, so what is on screen is what is in
        // the database.
        _draft = null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the reader is at the newest line, which is what decides if an arriving message
    /// scrolls the list. True when there is no module to ask — a conversation that has not been
    /// rendered yet is one nobody has scrolled away from.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task<bool> IsAtBottomAsync()
    {
        if (_module is null)
        {
            return true;
        }

        try
        {
            return await _module.InvokeAsync<bool>("isAtBottom", _listId);
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // The browser is gone, so there is no reader whose place could be kept.
            return true;
        }
        catch (JSException)
        {
            // Anything else the browser answered with. This is awaited inside a [JSInvokable], so an
            // exception escaping here is an unhandled one on the circuit's own dispatcher and takes
            // the whole screen down - over a question about a scroll position.
            return true;
        }
    }

    private async Task ScrollAsync()
    {
        if (_module is null)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await _module.InvokeVoidAsync("scrollToNewest", _listId);
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // Nothing to scroll: the browser is gone.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The resource key behind a refusal that arrived over the hub.
    /// <para>
    /// A <c>HubException</c>'s message crosses the wire verbatim, and the hub puts exactly one of
    /// AD-8's keys in it; the JavaScript client wraps that in its own sentence on the way back. So
    /// the key is looked for rather than parsed out, and anything unrecognised is the unexpected
    /// failure it is rather than a raw string put in front of a user (NFR-3).
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string KeyOf(JSException failure) =>
        Enum.GetValues<ErrorCode>()
            .Select(code => code.ToString())
            .FirstOrDefault(name => failure.Message.Contains(name, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        ?? nameof(ErrorCode.COMMON_UNEXPECTED_ERROR);
}
